WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.320
Here you see an incredible new robot&nbsp;
that learned to explore, stand up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:13.400
and even handle packages. And more. Now, wait a&nbsp;
second. What you see here should be impossible!

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:18.560
So, why is that? Why is this&nbsp;
impossible? Dear Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:21.680
this is Two Minute Papers&nbsp;
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:28.800
Well, to understand what is going on here, let’s&nbsp;
look into the popular Large Language Models first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:35.400
You see, when training a large language model&nbsp;
to understand English and be a good assistant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.880
we can give it tons and tons of data from&nbsp;
the internet to learn on. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.720
the early GPT-2 read Amazon reviews,&nbsp;
and learned to write new ones. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.800
was possible because tons and tons&nbsp;
of training data was available.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:58.960
However, this should be impossible for a real&nbsp;
robot in the real world. Why is that? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:05.520
have a look at this paper, where scientists at&nbsp;
NVIDIA tried to teach a software agent to run.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:13.080
And it kind of learned to run, okay, but after&nbsp;
quite a bit of training. At first, it looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:19.920
like this. Now letting a robot loose in the lab&nbsp;
while flailing around would not work too well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:26.200
because it would injure itself and its environment&nbsp;
before any meaningful learning could happen.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:32.680
So in software there is plenty of training&nbsp;
data to learn from, but for robotics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:41.360
there is no data. None. Or even if there is,&nbsp;
there is just not enough to learn anything. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:49.760
how can we solve this? Well, the solution is my&nbsp;
favorite. Which is learning inside a simulation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:56.000
Letting the robot play around in a video game.&nbsp;
Here, they are doing reinforcement learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.680
which means truly playing a video game.&nbsp;
Here, you do something, and if you do well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:09.440
you get a score. A reward. If you don’t do so&nbsp;
well, you get nothing, or a negative score.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:16.160
Now, we also need a little magic sauce here to&nbsp;
make sure that the robot is curious and wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:22.200
to explore and understand the world around&nbsp;
it. Normally, that is not that difficult,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:28.800
for instance, DeepMind’s agents can play video&nbsp;
games as they are yearning for a high score,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:38.440
so they will go around and explore. However,&nbsp;
there is a huge problem. A TV problem. Yes, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:46.560
heard it right. The AI has a TV problem. Agents&nbsp;
like these can encounter a TV in a virtual world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:54.600
and then, this happens. Yes, they get addicted&nbsp;
to it, and they never want to leave. Now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.600 --> 00:03:00.520
is real human-like behavior if I’ve ever seen&nbsp;
one! This happens because the agent is getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:07.520
new information all the time and it’s much more&nbsp;
interesting than these otherwise boring levels.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:13.400
So, how did they make this little robot&nbsp;
curious? Well, by engineering the rewards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:20.160
of the game in a way that for instance, if&nbsp;
the angle or the velocity of the door changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:26.400
it gets a reward. Thus, look! It&nbsp;
starts experimenting with it. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:31.120
new task! If we wish to get it to move&nbsp;
boxes around to where they belong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:37.920
we can craft a reward involving the velocity of&nbsp;
the box, and the distance from the container.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.400
Now hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;
because this is the moment of truth. Let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:51.000
jump from the video game world into the real&nbsp;
world, and see how this little robot fares. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:58.360
whoa! Look at that. It can navigate around&nbsp;
in the real world competently, it can even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:06.040
stand up. It now opens those doors, and can&nbsp;
handle packages. Well, as you see, it handles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:12.960
them at least as gently as some of the delivery&nbsp;
services out there. Another human-like trait.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:19.560
Perfect! Now jokes aside, by further tailoring&nbsp;
these reward functions, I would imagine that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:26.400
could be made much gentler with these packages,&nbsp;
and even doors just one more paper down the line.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:33.520
And also, bravo, what has been achieved&nbsp;
here is extremely hard. Why? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:40.000
because we cannot just make a simpler video&nbsp;
game for this robot to learn on. It has to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.600
good enough to even work in the real world&nbsp;
after all! And if the knowledge from here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:52.760
does not translate properly to here, things can&nbsp;
break really quickly. This was an amazing feat.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:58.840
And just imagine that now, we have better&nbsp;
and better tools to create virtual worlds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:04.040
a lot of our episodes are on new papers&nbsp;
that improve these potential simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:09.320
environments. And every single one of&nbsp;
those could be one step closer for us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:15.800
to have these little AIs train in these virtual&nbsp;
worlds, and then come out into the real world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:23.040
help us out and do all this safely. For instance,&nbsp;
these little robots could help us with last-mile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:28.400
delivery. The idea behind this paper could&nbsp;
also help us create better self-driving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:34.640
cars that can safely train in an environment&nbsp;
where we can make crazy hard levels for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:41.360
and with a powerful computer, have it train for&nbsp;
years and years in computer simulation time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:49.840
not in real time, and then come out as a safe and&nbsp;
competent AI agent. What a time to be alive! And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:57.560
this is not science fiction. Not at all. In an&nbsp;
earlier paper, we saw NVIDIA’s AI train for 33&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.560 --> 00:06:04.720
years in simulation time to play Minecraft,&nbsp;
and boy, did it learn a great deal in there.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:10.680
Now, this new work, of course does not&nbsp;
come without limitations. Hand-engineering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:16.600
reward functions is a bit of a limitation here.&nbsp;
Which means that we have to write these rewards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:23.160
ourselves. So, if we need it to perform a new&nbsp;
task, it needs a new reward function for that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:29.640
limiting the generality of the agent. An earlier&nbsp;
paper we talked about here could potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:36.200
remedy that as it is about teaching these AI&nbsp;
agents to find out what a good score would be.

